{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/38627-shiva-and-kali-aren-t-evil","id":38627,"title":"Shiva and Kali aren't evil","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2013-12-29T00:25:39-08:00","id":38627,"title":"Shiva and Kali aren't evil","content":"I can understand many of the mistakes that the creators made about Hinduism, such as Vishnu has been with many women not just Lakshmi as that is one of his allegorys about religion, but Kali and Shiva are not evil. In fact other than Vishnu they are some of the most popular Gods in Hinduism. Kali can be destructive, violently so, but she is a good god, and I have no idea how they decided Shiva was evil except that his part in the universe is destroyer of Worlds which isn't an evil thing at all if you understand how it is meant. yes Shiva beheaded his son Ganesh, but that was because he had no idea he was his son and thought he was actually hurting his wife who had told Ganesh to stand guard while she bathed. As soon as he found out about his misunderstanding, his tears and sorrow prompted an elephant to loan his head for Ganesh. Which has a religious meaning as well. I feel that Kali and Shiva were just portrayed with almost no understanding of who they are at all. Take a Kali, she is supposed to be frightening...sometimes. But she is known as a slayer of evil, a protector, a mother, and even sometimes one of the most loving goddesses. It felt like they felt they had to put some evil god in there and just assigned it to them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1836-jayna","name":"Jayna","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-29T00:35:37-08:00","id":38628,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jayna</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/38627-shiva-and-kali-aren-t-evil#post38627\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It felt like they felt they had to put some evil god in there and just assigned it to them.</div> </div> </div> </div>That. They did that.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm a professor! Why is no one listening to me?!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/235-telgar","name":"Telgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-29T02:02:00-08:00","id":38638,"title":"","content":"Well there were better options than them, it was horrible. How do you take such two beloved gods and twist them until they are no longer them? I mean that would be like saying Jesus was sent to earth to purify His sins. You make Shiva and Kali evil and it isn't Hinduism anymore.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1836-jayna","name":"Jayna","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-29T03:28:15-08:00","id":38659,"title":"","content":"But research is <i>hard</i>, Jayna. You have to <i>read</i> or <i>talk to people</i>. Freelancers don't have time for that.<br /> <br /> <br /> No, seriously, you're absolutely correct. It's not just Hinduism, most of the Pantheons in Scion are poorly handled. It gets worse the further they get away from Western pop-culture. The Egyptians and Greeks are mostly fine, the Irish and Norse are iffy. After that, it's just tragedy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm a professor! Why is no one listening to me?!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/235-telgar","name":"Telgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-29T05:19:53-08:00","id":38685,"title":"","content":"I was born and raised a Hindu in Kolkata, which is Kali's sacred city and the centre of her worship. Kali is my Ista-Devi, which is Hinduism shorthand for favourite God (though it holds far deeper meaning than just that), so suffice to say that I have more than some passing knowledge of the subject. That being said, I don't find Companion's Kali write-up to be portraying her as evil so much as only showing one side of her. Kali is indeed, a fearsome, terrible Goddess...to her enemies. To her devotees Kali is the universal Mama Bear, willing to do absolutely anything to protect her cubs. I feel a more well-rounded description of Kali in 2e would help explain her nature to an audience that primarily knows her from Temple of Doom.<br /> <br /> No, it is Shiva's description that I find insulting! Shiva is a God whose very name means 'The Auspicious One'. Out of the Trimurti, Shiva is the kindest, most caring and overall nicest. He is noted as being the God who is easiest to please amongst the whole Pantheon, and the one most willing to forgive those who transgress against him. He has a temper, it is true, but he is always quick to make amends when he calms down. Compared to Brahma, who actively lies and cheats to try and assert his superiority over the other Trimurti, and Vishnu whose stellar deeds include kicking his pregnant wife into exile because his popularity polls were dropping, cheating on Lakshmi with Goddesses like Prithvi and Tulsi, and forcibly ending, with trickery, the rule of a King so just he is still worshipped in some parts of India as a God, Shiva is a Saint.<br /> <br /> And what does Companion paint him as? A mean-tempered tantrum-prone drug addict who enjoys cheating on Parvati!<br /> <br /> In general, I will say the Hindu God chapter could use some revision. Personally, I find it shocking that a culture that stands with Dharma as its backbone does not have the Duty Virtue!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-29T06:10:55-08:00","id":38695,"title":"","content":"It should be noted that that the writers were trying to deal with the Vedic-period interpretations of the Hindu Gods, not necessarily the forms they currently have in modern Hindu practice. At least, I think that was the idea. It kinda ended up going both ways and managing to just muddy everything together into a big mess. The ancient forms can be pretty different.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm a professor! Why is no one listening to me?!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/235-telgar","name":"Telgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-29T06:29:16-08:00","id":38703,"title":"","content":"If that was the intent, I must admit I feel it could have been better done. Personally, I would prefer if the Deva were based on Classical Hinduism as opposed to Vedic, since I believe having the likes of Kali and Ganesha, who are Classical era enriches the setting. Plus the clearer distinctions between the Deva and Ahura that exist during Classical Hinduism brings in the Deva vs Yazata angle. I will note, though, that while Shiva's Vedic form, Rudra, is considerably less approachable, he is still not actively malicious. At worst, he is the bringer of disease as a natural part of the world, but also a supreme healer who protects against the same. I will also note that the idea of Shiva as a nice guy is not exactly a modern invention. In fact, Shiva's sect of Hinduism, Shaivism, is older than Vaishnavism, the Vishnu worshipping sect that has been immortalized in the American mind in the form of the Hare Krishnas.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-29T07:24:58-08:00","id":38721,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samudra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/38627-shiva-and-kali-aren-t-evil?p=38703#post38703\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If that was the intent, I must admit I feel it could have been better done. Personally, I would prefer if the Deva were based on Classical Hinduism as opposed to Vedic, since I believe having the likes of Kali and Ganesha, who are Classical era enriches the setting. Plus the clearer distinctions between the Deva and Ahura that exist during Classical Hinduism brings in the Deva vs Yazata angle. I will note, though, that while Shiva's Vedic form, Rudra, is considerably less approachable, he is still not actively malicious. At worst, he is the bringer of disease as a natural part of the world, but also a supreme healer who protects against the same. I will also note that the idea of Shiva as a nice guy is not exactly a modern invention. In fact, Shiva's sect of Hinduism, Shaivism, is older than Vaishnavism, the Vishnu worshipping sect that has been immortalized in the American mind in the form of the Hare Krishnas.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're coming in here with a lot of baggage. I sincerely doubt the typical American even makes the connection between Kali and Indiana Jones and the Temple of Doom, and I can think of virtually nobody that connects Vishnu with the Hare Krishna, whom most Americans are likely to dismiss as &quot;dirty hippies.&quot; Or Buddhists. Whichever is worse in their mind.<br /> <br /> Most Americans, of which a significant percentage come from Scandinavian stock, are only aware of Norse gods through Marvel comics. They're completely ignorant of the Celtic pantheons, comprising a large majority of the remaining Caucasian stock in America. So it's not like there's a premium on understanding pagan religions here that makes the lack of attention to Deva particularly egregious. If you're lucky you get an overview of Greek mythology (typically referred to as &quot;classical mythology&quot;) in school and probably spend more time discussing Martin Luther and Calvinism.<br /> <br /> Prior to college my introduction to the Deva was a single class in religious education and talking about Shiva with a friend whose family is Shaivist. Frankly, even after having spent a significant amount of time in South Asia I'm still poorly versed in Hinduism. There's a lot of ground to cover, comparatively, and so many sects that it makes studying it and distilling some &quot;true Hinduism&quot; a professional scholarly career (or ten), not something you do in the process of freelancing for an RPG.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/889-dayen","name":"Dayen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar889_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"330"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-29T07:53:19-08:00","id":38733,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You're coming in here with a lot of baggage. I sincerely doubt the typical American even makes the connection between Kali and Indiana Jones and the Temple of Doom, and I can think of virtually nobody that connects Vishnu with the Hare Krishna, whom most Americans are likely to dismiss as &quot;dirty hippies.&quot; Or Buddhists. Whichever is worse in their mind. </div> </div> </div>Perhaps. I apologize for the tone of my earlier comments. I still think the Deva need some serious retooling, but that was not the best way to put forth my concerns.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Most Americans, of which a significant percentage come from Scandinavian stock, are only aware of Norse gods through Marvel comics. They're completely ignorant of the Celtic pantheons, comprising a large majority of the remaining Caucasian stock in America. So it's not like there's a premium on understanding pagan religions here that makes the lack of attention to Deva particularly egregious. If you're lucky you get an overview of Greek mythology (typically referred to as &quot;classical mythology&quot;) in school and probably spend more time discussing Martin Luther and Calvinism. </div> </div> </div>You misunderstand me. I am not claiming that Americans have an especial lack of knowledge about Hinduism. Gods know, some of my own countrymen (a small part, but sadly existent) are quick to sneer any time a foreigner sincerely wants to learn anything about it. It's just that Hindu mythology is the one I'm most familiar with, so any mistakes in it are most likely to bother me, just like an Aztec specialist would find portions of the Aztec God write-up very rankling. Which, now that I think about it, is the definition of having prior baggage, so, once again, I'm sorry about that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">Prior to college my introduction to the Deva was a single class in religious education and talking about Shiva with a friend whose family is Shaivist. Frankly, even after having spent a significant amount of time in South Asia I'm still poorly versed in Hinduism. There's a lot of ground to cover, comparatively, and so many sects that it makes studying it and distilling some &quot;true Hinduism&quot; a professional scholarly career (or ten), not something you do in the process of freelancing for an RPG.</span></span> </div> </div> </div> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\"><br /> I understand that there's a lot of ground to cover, and I'm not suggesting that they attempt it. In fact, how a religion treats its God is not always the best metric for judging his character, how <i>mythology</i> treats him is. No matter what Zeus did, the Ancient Greeks considered him the greatest guy ever. He's still a cheat and serial rapist. My dislike of Shiva's write-up comes from the fact that there are no myths where he comes off as a mean-tempered person always looking for a fight. It's like that particular write-up came up out of nowhere.</span><br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-29T08:44:43-08:00","id":38752,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samudra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/38627-shiva-and-kali-aren-t-evil?p=38733#post38733\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Perhaps. I apologize for the tone of my earlier comments. I still think the Deva need some serious retooling, but that was not the best way to put forth my concerns.</div> </div> </div> </div>No worries. I understand passions tend to run high with regards to one's deeply held beliefs. I just felt the conversation would benefit from your being more calm and collected.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samudra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/38627-shiva-and-kali-aren-t-evil?p=38733#post38733\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You misunderstand me. I am not claiming that Americans have an especial lack of knowledge about Hinduism. Gods know, some of my own countrymen (a small part, but sadly existent) are quick to sneer any time a foreigner sincerely wants to learn anything about it. It's just that Hindu mythology is the one I'm most familiar with, so any mistakes in it are most likely to bother me, just like an Aztec specialist would find portions of the Aztec God write-up very rankling. Which, now that I think about it, is the definition of having prior baggage, so, once again, I'm sorry about that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Americans have a remarkable capacity for ignorance; I say this as a 14th generation American. Trust me. I worked closely with one of our chaplains for a long time on inter-faith dialogue in Muslim countries because I had the strongest background in Islam. Which isn't to say that I am an Islamic scholar to any degree, simply that I could delineate the difference between Sufism, Wahhabism, and Salafism and touch on the particulars of the Qu'ran and Sunnah (and to a lesser extent the Hadith). This chaplain is a man I deeply respect for his unshakable faith and dedication to smoothing out religious conflicts. But he happened to catch sight of my dog tags one day and saw that mine listed Asatru as my religion. He didn't even know what it was and asked me. His prior exposure to the Aesir amounted to Marvel comics. Walking him through the major tenets of practice was harrowing. He became concerned that I was suicidal. I still regularly exchange e-mails with him and we talk about the broadening of religious horizons in the military, but I make sure to rib him now and again about his &quot;suicidal berserkers.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samudra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/38627-shiva-and-kali-aren-t-evil?p=38733#post38733\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In fact, how a religion treats its God is not always the best metric for judging his character, how <i>mythology</i> treats him is. No matter what Zeus did, the Ancient Greeks considered him the greatest guy ever. He's still a cheat and serial rapist. My dislike of Shiva's write-up comes from the fact that there are no myths where he comes off as a mean-tempered person always looking for a fight. It's like that particular write-up came up out of nowhere.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure you've got a solid grasp. Greeks respect Zeus as the king of the gods, not because he's a respectable fellow. The syllogism inherent is that one must honor authority and honor thy father (and that they are essentially one and the same). Norse prayed to Thor for strength and courage, even though he was widely held to be a giant douchenozzle. Because Thor could grant you strength and courage. Just like you prayed to Odin for victory and cunning, even if he had spent the last five years tormenting you with disasters. I suppose that's a fundamental East-West dichotomy in practice of faith.<br /> <br /> As far as where Shiva's write-up comes from, I'd say a lack of understanding as to Shiva's role as the destroyer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/889-dayen","name":"Dayen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar889_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"330"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-29T09:37:42-08:00","id":38765,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dayen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/38627-shiva-and-kali-aren-t-evil?p=38752#post38752\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not sure you've got a solid grasp. Greeks respect Zeus as the king of the gods, not because he's a respectable fellow. The syllogism inherent is that one must honor authority and honor thy father (and that they are essentially one and the same). Norse prayed to Thor for strength and courage, even though he was widely held to be a giant douchenozzle. Because Thor could grant you strength and courage. Just like you prayed to Odin for victory and cunning, even if he had spent the last five years tormenting you with disasters. I suppose that's a fundamental East-West dichotomy in practice of faith.<br /> <br /> As far as where Shiva's write-up comes from, I'd say a lack of understanding as to Shiva's role as the destroyer.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm...you might have hit the nail right on the head. The concept that I <i>must</i> worship a particular God because of whatever reason is foreign in Classical Hinduism, to the point that Atheist Hindus can and do exist. It's part of why Hinduism has no Pope equivalent. (Of course, that is all the ideal case. There are plenty of people around who would be more than happy to tell you how to be a good little Hindu).<br /> <br /> Anyway, I am drifting from the point of this thread! At the end of the day, I think 1e suffered a case of 'Creation Very Good, Preservation Good, Destruction Not So Good', that I hope is addressed in 2e.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-29T10:49:00-08:00","id":38790,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samudra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/38627-shiva-and-kali-aren-t-evil?p=38685#post38685\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was born and raised a Hindu in Kolkata, which is Kali's sacred city and the centre of her worship. Kali is my Ista-Devi, which is Hinduism shorthand for favourite God (though it holds far deeper meaning than just that), so suffice to say that I have more than some passing knowledge of the subject. That being said, I don't find Companion's Kali write-up to be portraying her as evil so much as only showing one side of her. Kali is indeed, a fearsome, terrible Goddess...to her enemies. To her devotees Kali is the universal Mama Bear, willing to do absolutely anything to protect her cubs. I feel a more well-rounded description of Kali in 2e would help explain her nature to an audience that primarily knows her from Temple of Doom.<br /> <br /> No, it is Shiva's description that I find insulting! Shiva is a God whose very name means 'The Auspicious One'. Out of the Trimurti, Shiva is the kindest, most caring and overall nicest. He is noted as being the God who is easiest to please amongst the whole Pantheon, and the one most willing to forgive those who transgress against him. He has a temper, it is true, but he is always quick to make amends when he calms down. Compared to Brahma, who actively lies and cheats to try and assert his superiority over the other Trimurti, and Vishnu whose stellar deeds include kicking his pregnant wife into exile because his popularity polls were dropping, cheating on Lakshmi with Goddesses like Prithvi and Tulsi, and forcibly ending, with trickery, the rule of a King so just he is still worshipped in some parts of India as a God, Shiva is a Saint.<br /> <br /> And what does Companion paint him as? A mean-tempered tantrum-prone drug addict who enjoys cheating on Parvati!<br /> <br /> In general, I will say the Hindu God chapter could use some revision. Personally, I find it shocking that a culture that stands with Dharma as its backbone does not have the Duty Virtue!</div> </div> </div> </div>My main issue with how they did Kali, is it seemed almost entirely based on the Western concept of her. And like it or not most Americans only understanding of Kali IS the temple of doom, or the thugee which was completely a misunderstanding of history anyway. Without showing her loving side, they portrayed her in more evocative terms. They were implying it in a way and without explaining she isnt fully how they described. It was a horrible way to handle her.<br /> <br /> I agree about Shiva, to show him as some sort of evil cruel God, and to say he cut off Ganesh's head without explaining the details? It was ridiculous. I agree that many of their mistakes I can put up to lack of research, but making Shiva evil was out of nowhere and made no sense whatsoever in any part of Hinduism.<br /> <br /> Although, I think Shiva would be one of the first to honor Vishnu, just as Vishnu prays to him. there was a whole lot more to Sita's exile than just popularity, and his &quot;cheating&quot; is a lot more than just that as well. But I agree Shiva is truly a saint and to believe he is evil just boggles how anyone can believe that","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1836-jayna","name":"Jayna","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-29T11:49:49-08:00","id":38802,"title":"","content":"I wouldn't claim to know much about Hinduism; my research on the subject has chiefly been how it relates to India's magic. But the Deva chapter made me wince, it was so blatantly ignorant. <br /> <br /> One thing I know about Hinduism: It's a <i>very big subject.</i> It's too much to ask of a freelancer to tackle Hindu mythology &quot;cold&quot; and produce a game adaptation in a few months. <br /> <br /> I sympathize with whoever had to try. I did the Celestial Bureaucracy chapter: I researched as intensely as I could, and had the advantage of some prior knowledge of Chinese myth and magic so I wasn't starting completely from scratch, but I was keenly aware that my information was horribly incomplete. Chinese mythology is also a very big subject, and there seems to be very little material in English that comes from an actual Chinese perspective. I can only hope that I managed to grasp a few key concepts, sort of, loosely. (Though it did look like Chinese popular culture takes a rather loose and freewheeling approach to its own mythology, so I may hope that any Chinese readers didn't find it too laughable or insulting. If I had it to do over again, though -- as writer or developer -- I'd be enormously grateful to get the perspective of people who grew up in the culture, not just books on the subject.) <br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-29T12:08:01-08:00","id":38806,"title":"","content":"The still-living religions present another problem that makes treating them respectfully kinda hard: <b>they're still around</b>. That means they're still evolving, changing and you can't 'snapshot' them as easily as you can the ones that aren't active in their original forms. All respect to reconstructionist religions, but those aren't the original forms of the religion which, in most cases, we know <i>very</i> little about. It's a lot easier to look at dynastic Egyptian religion from a perspective of almost 2000 years after the end of contiguous native practice. There's no ancient Egyptians left to tell you you're doing it wrong.<br /> <br /> There are still issues with looking at the thousands of years of religions evolution and trying to sort out what the gods would look like &quot;now&quot;, with layers and layers of changes built into them. Do you say that anything after the start of Hellenization in Egypt 'doesn't count' for Egyptian religion? Sure, you can do that and get away with it pretty easily, but it's totally a judgement call on your part. Egyptian religion, like all religions, shifted its portrayal of different figures based on current politics and cultural perspectives. As one part of the empire grew stronger, the gods favored by that portion became more important, while the disenfranchised gods grew less important or were cast in a negative light. Sometimes Set was super awesome and heroic, sometimes he was a total bastard. It depends on when you're looking at, who you're asking.<br /> <br /> Religion, which <i>all mythologies are</i>, is super complicated and never clear-cut. No religion is monolithic and codified, offering an exact map of the One True Way to read it. Every little sect of a religion has their own One True Way and they get really touchy about it being challenged. After all, it's <i>their religion</i>. Kinda important to most people. <br /> <br /> The best Scion can hope for is to pick a period in time, snapshot the religion at that point and try to do a decent gloss-over on key figures. If they even get as accurate as a children's mythology book, I'd be pretty happy. That's just concerning accuracy of things like Associations, basic descriptions and Favors. Scion is, after all, trying to construct a single, cohesive world setting that incorporates 10+ entirely <i>non-compatible</i> cosmologies. It's going to take some liberties with reality, here.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm a professor! Why is no one listening to me?!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/235-telgar","name":"Telgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-29T12:38:30-08:00","id":38825,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DShomshak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/38627-shiva-and-kali-aren-t-evil?p=38802#post38802\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I wouldn't claim to know much about Hinduism; my research on the subject has chiefly been how it relates to India's magic. But the Deva chapter made me wince, it was so blatantly ignorant.<br /> <br /> One thing I know about Hinduism: It's a <i>very big subject.</i> It's too much to ask of a freelancer to tackle Hindu mythology &quot;cold&quot; and produce a game adaptation in a few months.<br /> <br /> I sympathize with whoever had to try. I did the Celestial Bureaucracy chapter: I researched as intensely as I could, and had the advantage of some prior knowledge of Chinese myth and magic so I wasn't starting completely from scratch, but I was keenly aware that my information was horribly incomplete. Chinese mythology is also a very big subject, and there seems to be very little material in English that comes from an actual Chinese perspective. I can only hope that I managed to grasp a few key concepts, sort of, loosely. (Though it did look like Chinese popular culture takes a rather loose and freewheeling approach to its own mythology, so I may hope that any Chinese readers didn't find it too laughable or insulting. If I had it to do over again, though -- as writer or developer -- I'd be enormously grateful to get the perspective of people who grew up in the culture, not just books on the subject.)<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak</div> </div> </div> </div>You just have to look at the co-opting of historical figures as gods to see that it's not religion in the western sense. It's also not even a flavor of ancestor worship like Shinto, which makes it very unapproachable from the perspective of someone raised and operating in an Abrahamic tradition.<br /> <br /> I wouldn't be surprised if Mao Zhedong is considered Shen in 200 years.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/889-dayen","name":"Dayen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar889_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"330"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-29T13:53:12-08:00","id":38860,"title":"","content":"Hmm...Hindu magic;<br /> <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siddhi\" target=\"_blank\">Link one</a> from <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iddhipada\" target=\"_blank\">this one</a><br /> <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iddhi\" target=\"_blank\">Another Link </a><br /> <br /> And then there are those super <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astra_(weapon)\" target=\"_blank\">weapons and spells</a><br /> <br /> and it goes on and on.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-29T13:57:22-08:00","id":38862,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DShomshak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/38627-shiva-and-kali-aren-t-evil?p=38802#post38802\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I sympathize with whoever had to try. I did the Celestial Bureaucracy chapter: I researched as intensely as I could, and had the advantage of some prior knowledge of Chinese myth and magic so I wasn't starting completely from scratch, but I was keenly aware that my information was horribly incomplete. Chinese mythology is also a very big subject, and there seems to be very little material in English that comes from an actual Chinese perspective. I can only hope that I managed to grasp a few key concepts, sort of, loosely. (Though it did look like Chinese popular culture takes a rather loose and freewheeling approach to its own mythology, so I may hope that any Chinese readers didn't find it too laughable or insulting. If I had it to do over again, though -- as writer or developer -- I'd be enormously grateful to get the perspective of people who grew up in the culture, not just books on the subject.)</div> </div> </div> </div>It could've been better. Some of the choices were strange and some notable figures were missing, but on the plus side, it was clear that a honest effort was made to do the research despite there being few resources available in English. Considering some of the other write-ups for the line, you did very well under the circumstances.<br /> <br /> Particularly considering that the rest of Scion saw hilarious stuff such as a star-god leading the Titan-realm of Darkness, Fujin being called &quot;Wind of God&quot; instead of &quot;God of Wind,&quot; and the Aztec pantheon being called the Atzlanti even though the Aztec homeland is named Aztlan.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1607-creticus","name":"Creticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"69"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-29T15:37:30-08:00","id":38927,"title":"","content":"I can understand someone getting the idea that Kali is evil.<br /> <img src=\"http://www.exoticindia.com/oils/mother_goddess_mahakali_or99.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/><br /> <br /> I mean.. Look at her. She's fucking <i>Metal</i>. She accessorizes with Severed Heads.<br /> <br /> <br /> I would play the hell out of a Scion of Kali and be the craziest motherfucker in the party.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"color:orangered\">Duly Elected Guild Hierarch</span> of the Exalted Forum<br /> <a href=\"http://northernfoxgames.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://northernfoxgames.com/</a></b>","edited":{"userid":331,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/331-the-revenge-of-tv-head","user":"The Revenge of TV Head","date":". 12-30-2013, 06:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/331-the-revenge-of-tv-head","name":"The Revenge of TV Head","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar331_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1272"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-29T20:41:52-08:00","id":39080,"title":"","content":"Mao Zedong is considered Shen <i>right now</i> Or at least there are, so I'm told by journalists on the China beat, actual offerings made before pictures of him just as if he were a god. <br /> <br /> As Telgar says, you have to cherry-pick your mythic elements and maybe hammer on them a bit to turn them into a somewhat-coherent and playable game setting. The trick, I think, lies in developing an imaginative sympathy for the mythology: To creatively misunderstand the details (to use critic Harold Bloom's notion of &quot;creative misprision&quot;) while trying to stay true to the emotional core of what the stories mean to the people who tell them. Though it can be difficult when you look at a very big room through very small windows. <br /> <br /> For instance, I had some arguments with other writers about the CB's Virtues. Now, Virtues are a pretty rough way to encapsulate the essential attitudes of a mythology and the culture that creates it, but it's what Scion gives us. One of my first decisions was that the selection of Virtues that Scion: Hero gave to Tommy Li (p. 252) was just plain wrong. You can make a case for each of Duty, Harmony, Loyalty and Order, but the whole package is too one-sided. It seems to be a common image of China, but as I looked at how the heroes of actual Chinese legend and literature behave, I saw that they just aren't so stuffily Confucian. The Handsome Monkey King is Chaos incarnate. Nezha is an impetuous punk. Guan Yu begins his career by killing an oppressive landlord/local official and going on the run as an outlaw. There's something wrong if the CB Virtue set doesn't represent this rambunctious quality of so many Chinese heroes. So, Order goes and Valor takes its place. <br /> <br /> As a starting-point for a version of the Devas that doesn't suck, would anyone venture an opinion about what the Deva Virtues should be? If you feel ambitious, go down the list of Virtues and explain how each one does or doesn't apply to Hinduism and its heroic tales. <br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-29T22:50:44-08:00","id":39163,"title":"","content":"WARNING: Wall of text.<br /> <br /> Conviction: Conviction implies not only a 'I'm right' feeling but also a subtle 'you're wrong' along with that. As such, personal needs must bow to Duty. Not a good choice.<br /> <br /> Courage: Kali is the only real berserker in the Pantheon, and her rages are always things that eventually need to be stopped. Most of the Deva prefer to deal with their enemies through Intellect, like Vishnu's many escapades and Sarasvati's punking of Kumbhakarna. Even in war, an ideal set of rules is supposed to be followed, it's not a free-for-all.<br /> <br /> Duty: The Big One. Dharma trumps most everything in Hindu myth and culture. Bheeshma and Drona in the Mahabharata fight against their most beloved students because of Duty. Ekalavya cuts off his own thumb, rendering the best archer in the world unable to ever pick up a bow, because of Duty. King Dashrath agrees to send his favoured son into exile because of Duty. Hanuman fights his own son because of both their Duties. The list goes on.<br /> <br /> Endurance: While the image of the sadhu meditating in the mountaintop is probably enough to award Endurance to the Deva, it should be noted that the Deva display more than just the asceticism part of Endurance. They also display the Norse type of Endurance, of the non-stop party variety. Sure, Parvati spends eons without eating and Bhagirath meditates for many an age, but Ganesha eats and eats until his stomach bursts open, and Indra has epic drinking binges. Sarasvati holds marathon concerts, and Shiva dances till he drops (he doesn't drop). Endurance is also a shoe-in.<br /> <br /> Expression: I'm really not sure. India has a long tradition of arts and music, but then so do many other cultures. We do not, however, have a dedicated role in society dedicated to Expression like the Norse skalds or Irish bards. Then again, neither do the Greeks. Overall, I would not oppose it, but like I said, I'm not entirely sure as to what goes into awarding a Pantheon Expression.<br /> <br /> Harmony: Split down the middle. The Vedic Deva, being almost all nature deities, are heavily invested in Harmony. The post-Vedic Deva, however, not so much. I mean, the Trimurti and Co. Regularly allow their worshippers to hang out in the Overworld, despite the natural place for them being the Underworld. However, Harmony with nature is still an important part of Hindu culture, and I think this Virtue is a strong candidate, especially as a nod at the most ancient ideals of Hinduism.<br /> <br /> Intellect: An important one. Respect for knowledge and those who have it is an important part of Hindu culture, and even the Gods prefer to deal with foes with Intellect instead of brute force. This is to the point where sages and wise men regularly curse even the Gods in many myths. I'd say Intellect is quite appropriate.<br /> <br /> Loyalty: Like Valour below, Loyalty is a Virtue that is considered ideal, but ultimately bows to Duty. Vibheeshana in the Ramayana is lauded for his decision to go against his brother Ravana. Brothers Indra and Agni, and erstwhile best friends Drona and Drupad fight in the Mahabharata. Not an overall good choice.<br /> <br /> Order: A confusing one. For one, the upholding of laws and authority, while considered a good thing in the culture is not a giant symbol of it, the way the Pharaoh of Egypt or the Code of Hammurabi are. However, the concept of Universal Order, Rta, is quite important to the Vedic Deva, while not really much of a priority for the Trimurti and Co. As yet another consideration, almost all the Gods in charge of maintaining Rta (Mitra, Bhaga, Aryaman, Asura Mahat Varuna himself), are almost all Yazata. Overall, I'd say there are better Virtues.<br /> <br /> Piety: Hinduism is possibly the only religion that discusses atheism and considers it an equally valid way to enlightenment. As such, the belief in the superiority of your own Pantheon is non-existent, and Piety can be safely ignored.<br /> <br /> Valour: While fair, gentlemanly fights are considered ideal, there are a number of cases in Hindu myth when the heroes are willing to break the rules. The Kurukshetra War in the Mahabharata is a prime example. As such, Valour is not an ideal Virtue for the Deva.<br /> <br /> Vengeance: Not a Virtue often exhibited often. The only ones I can think of are Bali in the Ramayana and Gandhari at the end of the war in the Mahabharata, the former of which is always painted in a negative light. A Virtue that can be easily skipped.<br /> <br /> Going by all that, I'd say Duty, Endurance and Intellect are definitely Deva Virtues. The choice of fourth one is difficult, but I'd go with either Expression or Harmony.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-29T23:08:06-08:00","id":39180,"title":"","content":"Considering the amount of focus on poetry, dance and song in the Hindu stories I'm familiar with (but I'm not familiar with many), I'd suggest Expression. It seems to me, with my cursory knowledge, that there is a LOT of dancing and singing going on as a way to exert or express power.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm a professor! Why is no one listening to me?!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/235-telgar","name":"Telgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-30T02:04:41-08:00","id":39253,"title":"","content":"Near as I can tell, keeping one's word is a pretty damn big thing in Hindu. Not entirely sure which Virtue that falls under.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"color:orangered\">Duly Elected Guild Hierarch</span> of the Exalted Forum<br /> <a href=\"http://northernfoxgames.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://northernfoxgames.com/</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/331-the-revenge-of-tv-head","name":"The Revenge of TV Head","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar331_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1272"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-30T02:20:41-08:00","id":39259,"title":"","content":"I think it would fall under Duty. You have a responsibility to stay true to your word, and a duty to fulfil all your promises.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-30T03:32:38-08:00","id":39286,"title":"","content":"I think Greeks get the nod in Expression for their writers, playwrights, and philosophers. Food for thought if you want to make an argument for the Deva Expression based on the totality of Indian contributions to the world's knowledge.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/889-dayen","name":"Dayen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar889_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"330"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-30T04:45:41-08:00","id":39311,"title":"","content":"India's greatest contribution to the World, the invention of zero, seems to me to fall firmly in the realm of Intellect. That being said, I am increasingly beginning to feel that Expression might be the better choice. Telgar is right: A number of the Gods are mentioned as being expert artists. I mean, there's the obvious, Sarasvati, but Shiva is supposed to be an excellent instrumentalist along with the Lord of the Dance. The image of Krishna playing the flute is pretty central to his followers. Both great Epics and Kalidasa's Shakuntala are on the list of 100 greatest books ever-written, and Indian music has had some popularity on the world stage through Pt. Ravi Shankar's collaborations with Yehudi Menuhin and George Harrison. And perhaps most importantly, the arts were/are pretty important parts of the fabric of Indian culture. Mythology describes music as a gift of the Gods to Man and a vehicle for enlightenment, to the point that certain melodies are considered out and out capable of magic, kind of like Finnish mythology.<br /> <br /> At the end of the day, if you're going to focus purely on the Vedic era, then a case might be made for Harmony, but if you are going to include the Trimurti and other Gods of the Classical era, then my personal choice for Deva Virtues would be: Duty, Endurance, Expression and Intellect.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-30T06:00:34-08:00","id":39339,"title":"","content":"Endurance is definitely there.<br /> <br /> I think almost every battle/war they were ever involved in with other nations they always lost. However, despite those other nations' diminishment through time, India is standing a little more taller today as a nuclear armed nation.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-30T12:01:40-08:00","id":39519,"title":"","content":"A pantheon gets Expression if poetry, music, literature, etc. are vehicles of power. It isn't just that the culture has art -- everybody has art -- but that art is itself valued so much it becomes a form of power. Sounds like Hinduism qualifies. <br /> <br /> Biggest example of Endurance I can think of is Shiva's throat. At the Churning of the Ocean at the beginning of the world, there's a flood of poison so deadly it could destroy the nascent universe. So Shiva drinks it -- but he can't <i>swallow</i> it. It's so dangerous it would destroy even him! He holds it suspended in his throat for all time, neither out in the World nor reaching his stomach. Shiva's throat is pictured as tinted blue to show the presence of this poison. Presumably Shiva does not find this pleasant, but the Supreme Ascetic will hold the poison there forever so the World may continue to exist. <br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-30T12:27:25-08:00","id":39529,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Revenge of TV Head</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/38627-shiva-and-kali-aren-t-evil?p=38927#post38927\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I can understand someone getting the idea that Kali is evil.<br /> <img src=\"http://images5.fanpop.com/image/photos/31400000/Mahakali-gods-of-hinduism-31464472-900-668.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/><br /> <br /> I mean.. Look at her. She's fucking <i>Metal</i>. She accessorizes with Severed Heads.<br /> <br /> <br /> I would play the hell out of a Scion of Kali and be the craziest motherfucker in the party.</div> </div> </div> </div>I mean that is fine, anyone can have an evil scion, but Kali isnt evil, and if we suddenly say she is, than all the greeks should be the titans. Goodness knows they actually are evil on the most part, and if we just want to assign evilness due to that, I dont see how it is a mythology game anymore. I mean that is calling Thor in marvel comics the real Thor, which is nothing wrong if you want to play a game like that, but if you want to call one of the biggest Gods of a religion evil, than I dont see why any research is necessary for any of them.<br /> <br /> I really dont see why we cant assign random modifiers on all of them? I dont mind the vast majority of mistakes, but if 2 of the major Gods cant be anything like they actually are, than why isnt Zeus a celibate? Or Artemis the goddess of love? Or Thor the God of Mice? or Loki the God of Peace?<br /> <br /> Kali being evil isnt even trying.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1836-jayna","name":"Jayna","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-30T12:31:25-08:00","id":39532,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DShomshak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/38627-shiva-and-kali-aren-t-evil?p=39519#post39519\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A pantheon gets Expression if poetry, music, literature, etc. are vehicles of power. It isn't just that the culture has art -- everybody has art -- but that art is itself valued so much it becomes a form of power. Sounds like Hinduism qualifies.<br /> <br /> Biggest example of Endurance I can think of is Shiva's throat. At the Churning of the Ocean at the beginning of the world, there's a flood of poison so deadly it could destroy the nascent universe. So Shiva drinks it -- but he can't <i>swallow</i> it. It's so dangerous it would destroy even him! He holds it suspended in his throat for all time, neither out in the World nor reaching his stomach. Shiva's throat is pictured as tinted blue to show the presence of this poison. Presumably Shiva does not find this pleasant, but the Supreme Ascetic will hold the poison there forever so the World may continue to exist.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks for the clarification! Yes, by that standard Hinduism qualifies. There exist legends of men summoning storms and setting people on fire with nothing but songs. Add to that the fact that Shiva's Tandava dance is powerful enough to destroy the universe if done right, and that Sarasvati's music is what inspires and empowers Brahma in his daily act of Creation, and Expression is a shoe-in.<br /> <br /> That is about the best example of Endurance for the Deva that I can think of. Overall I am feeling pretty happy with the Duty, Endurance, Expression and Intellect Virtue set. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Anybody have any other suggestions/questions?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-30T18:25:44-08:00","id":39723,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samudra</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/38627-shiva-and-kali-aren-t-evil?p=39532#post39532\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Anybody have any other suggestions/questions?</div> </div> </div> </div>Get some of the Gotham By Night people and convince them of those virtues! Worst case scenario we have a very nice Deva based debate.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/484-iry","name":"Iry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar484_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"346"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-31T07:27:35-08:00","id":39962,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Iry</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/38627-shiva-and-kali-aren-t-evil?p=39723#post39723\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Get some of the Gotham By Night people and convince them of those virtues! Worst case scenario we have a very nice Deva based debate.</div> </div> </div> </div>I have actually already discussed the Deva Virtues, and my dissatisfaction with the current crop, with Anne and Jacob, in the comments section <a href=\"http://johnsscionresources.blogspot.in/2013/05/deva-decisions.html\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> and <a href=\"http://johnsscionresources.blogspot.in/2013/08/the-golden-age.html\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>. Anne has been very supportive of the idea of Duty as a core Deva Virtue. I have not spoken to her about Expression though, since it was only while discussing it in this thread that I came to the conclusion that it fits the Deva perfectly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-04T14:25:10-08:00","id":42480,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Iry</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/38627-shiva-and-kali-aren-t-evil?p=39723#post39723\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Get some of the Gotham By Night people and convince them of those virtues! Worst case scenario we have a very nice Deva based debate.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> He did <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Now we must think","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1051-jsr","name":"JSR","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1051_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-04T17:59:31-08:00","id":42608,"title":"","content":"I don't know much about the heroes of the Vedas, but weren't they running around on magical UFOs and throwing magic atom bombs at each other like the mythological equivalent of Dragonball Z? Shouldn't there be rules for this sort of thing? If I'm going to play one of their Scions, that's the sort of thing I'd want to be able to do, at least once I hit Demigod level.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1878-adamantinerapier","name":"AdamantineRapier","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1878_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"89"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-05T10:59:18-08:00","id":43007,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AdamantineRapier</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/38627-shiva-and-kali-aren-t-evil?p=42608#post42608\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't know much about the heroes of the Vedas, but weren't they running around on magical UFOs and throwing magic atom bombs at each other like the mythological equivalent of Dragonball Z? Shouldn't there be rules for this sort of thing? If I'm going to play one of their Scions, that's the sort of thing I'd want to be able to do, at least once I hit Demigod level.</div> </div> </div> </div>The 'UFOs' are called Pushpak Vimanas (or sometimes just Vimanas), and are a lot less common in Hindu mythology than some people give them credit for. However, it should not be a particularly difficult Relic to design, since it is, for all intents and purposes, a very fast flying chariot.<br /> <br /> The 'magic atom bombs' you speak of are actually a whole host of missile weapons, including the Brahmastra, the Pashupatastra, the Sudarshan Chakra, the Vajrastra, Vasavi etc. (Hindu Gods LOVE named superweapons). These are only granted for use to exceptional Scions under exceptional circumstances, usually with a whole host of riders as to their use. While I agree that rules for creating such weapons would be welcome, no Scion should <i><b>own</b></i> one until he hits God level, unless the storyteller really knows what he is doing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-05T11:39:28-08:00","id":43030,"title":"","content":"How about another repost...<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadowflame</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/38627-shiva-and-kali-aren-t-evil?p=38860#post38860\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hmm...Hindu magic;<br /> <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siddhi\" target=\"_blank\">Link one</a> from <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iddhipada\" target=\"_blank\">this one</a><br /> <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iddhi\" target=\"_blank\">Another Link </a><br /> <br /> And then there are those super <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Astra_(weapon)\" target=\"_blank\">weapons and spells</a><br /> <br /> and it goes on and on.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-12T18:28:39-08:00","id":47776,"title":"","content":"Another thing that 1e, to my knowledge, got wrong about the Devas is that Kali is called Shiva and Parvati's daughter, when, from what I understand, in Hindu mythology, she's actually one of Devi's incarnations, like Parvati and Durga, which leads me to a question I have: is it &quot;wrong&quot;, per se, to have different incarnations of the same deity be different options as a Scion's patron in the game. My knowledge of Hinduism is novice at best, so I could be wrong, but from what I've read, the different incarnations of Devi are prayed to for different things, so I think it probably would be okay, and personally, I wouldn't mind there being the option to play a Scion of Durga in 2e, but like I said, I don't know much about the Hindu religion compared to others who have posted on this thread, so I'd like to know your answers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/990-maplesamurai","name":"MapleSamurai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar990_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-12T19:29:10-08:00","id":47806,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MapleSamurai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/38627-shiva-and-kali-aren-t-evil?p=47776#post47776\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another thing that 1e, to my knowledge, got wrong about the Devas is that Kali is called Shiva and Parvati's daughter, when, from what I understand, in Hindu mythology, she's actually one of Devi's incarnations, like Parvati and Durga, which leads me to a question I have: is it &quot;wrong&quot;, per se, to have different incarnations of the same deity be different options as a Scion's patron in the game. My knowledge of Hinduism is novice at best, so I could be wrong, but from what I've read, the different incarnations of Devi are prayed to for different things, so I think it probably would be okay, and personally, I wouldn't mind there being the option to play a Scion of Durga in 2e, but like I said, I don't know much about the Hindu religion compared to others who have posted on this thread, so I'd like to know your answers.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't see it as wrong. I mean, the Shinto faith has this with O-Inari-sama, who is also a good 10 other gods in the pantheon, including Izanagi no Mikoto, Izanami no Mikoto, Ame no Uzume no Mikoto, Benten, Daikoku, Benzaiten, and others. I don't see anything wrong with going 'I'm the child of O-Inari in the aspect of Ame no Uzume no Mikoto', or 'I am the child of O-Inari in the aspect of Izanagi'. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1976-tashiro","name":"Tashiro","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1976_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-12T19:39:18-08:00","id":47813,"title":"","content":"Going back to the original post - I have to agree - Shiva and Kali <i>aren't </i>evil. This is something that is reflected upon with Shinto, too - there are no <i>evil </i>gods. A god is someone who supports society, and acts in accordance to the natural world. A god of storms who wipes out a village doesn't do it out of malice or with evil intent. It is the nature of the storm to sweep in from time to time - natural calamities are seen as a part of the order of life at best, or a sign that there is an imbalance in some fashion caused by mortal activity.<br /> <br /> According to Shinto (and from what I've seen, Vodou and Hinduism), good and evil are not something dealt with on a celestial scale. There's no cosmic good or cosmic evil. Evil is decided upon by society, and it is society's role to protect civilization and promote 'good'. It is not the purpose of religion to dictate what is Good or Evil, only what is harmonious, what brings spiritual balance. This has the ... perhaps uncomfortable... side-effect of stating 'you are responsible for your own actions'. You can't claim a higher force has 'forced' you to act in this fashion, if you perform an evil act, it is on your head, and nobody else's.<br /> <br /> This also has the side effect of altering the perception of how the world works. A violent god is obviously angered by some action performed by mortal society. It is the job of the faithful to discover what angered the god, to appease the god, and then find the person responsible and have them show contrition. The god isn't in the wrong - not as we understand it. Instead, the god acts in accordance with its nature. As far as storytelling is concerned, it becomes a lot less about 'beating up enemy gods and monsters', as it is about 'understanding why things are happening, and doing what needs to be done'. Sure, with actual monsters, you'll see battles (Susano-O vs Orochi comes to mind), but the gods in these pantheons didn't wage war on one another, or on people, and none were 'evil'.<br /> <br /> I think this is one of the benefits of the Shinto, Hindu, and Vodou faiths over some of the other ones (Norse and Egyptian come to mind). Being from a region which didn't see a lot of internal strife, there's no influence to make any specific god &quot;evil&quot; because they were revered by a rival faith (look at Loki and Set). I think if I'm going to run Scion, I'm removing the entire idea of the gods being 'good' or 'evil'. They are primeval forces, but ultimately, they're there to promote civilization and are a part of the natural world.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1976-tashiro","name":"Tashiro","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1976_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-12T19:54:45-08:00","id":47819,"title":"","content":"*shrugs*<br /> That's consistent with most of the pantheons from ancient myths. Order was always the concern. Anything that challenged the order was considered evil. An order that reinforced ideas such as slavery and a caste system.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-12T22:48:15-08:00","id":47944,"title":"","content":"That would be for humanity to figure out - and was not enforced by the gods. The Shinto pantheon does not care about order or chaos, either - and some of the gods of Shinto are in fact quite content sowing chaos (Susano-O is a good example here). Susano-O isn't considered evil, and neither is the Monkey King - two figures that would be considered chaotic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1976-tashiro","name":"Tashiro","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1976_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-13T05:38:40-08:00","id":48094,"title":"","content":"I think you're confusing the word &quot;order&quot; with the &quot;alignment&quot; system that some games have. Certain chaotic figures had their place. But any upset in the establishment typically meant misery for everyone, or so ancient priests would have us believe. (Some justification there I guess. Since anarchy is bad for progress)<br /> Of course, this really sucks to put this into a game, but for historical context I should think it is interesting. The ancient titans represented an older system, an order that had to make way for the new.<br /> Compassion, kindness, empathy, etc. are higher ideals that came later. Or perhaps it might be said that such ideals make a reappearance, as it's said that matriarchal societies pre-existed before the male-dominated ones we know now.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-13T07:46:14-08:00","id":48161,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MapleSamurai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/38627-shiva-and-kali-aren-t-evil?p=47776#post47776\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another thing that 1e, to my knowledge, got wrong about the Devas is that Kali is called Shiva and Parvati's daughter, when, from what I understand, in Hindu mythology, she's actually one of Devi's incarnations, like Parvati and Durga, which leads me to a question I have: is it &quot;wrong&quot;, per se, to have different incarnations of the same deity be different options as a Scion's patron in the game. My knowledge of Hinduism is novice at best, so I could be wrong, but from what I've read, the different incarnations of Devi are prayed to for different things, so I think it probably would be okay, and personally, I wouldn't mind there being the option to play a Scion of Durga in 2e, but like I said, I don't know much about the Hindu religion compared to others who have posted on this thread, so I'd like to know your answers.</div> </div> </div> </div>It is perfectly acceptable <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> This is an area where Hindu religion and Hindu mythology diverge somewhat. While the religion treats ALL Goddesses as manifestations of Shakti, the Supreme Feminine Energy of the universe, the mythology only pays lip service to this concept. There a re multiple scenes of the various Goddesses interacting with each other as separate beings, like the time Lakshmi and Sarasvati got into a quarrel over who is superior. Even Parvati and Kali, who are often seen as the light and dark sides of the same being, treat each other as separate, such as the time when Shiva teases Parvati about letting herself go and starting to look like Kali, and Parvati is horrified by the idea.<br /> <br /> I would say the ideal case would be to treat the older Vedic Goddesses (Ratri, Usha, Indrani, Svaha, Sanjana, Chaya etc.) as minor Goddesses in the Legend 9-10 range (the first two in that list might be bumped higher). All the post-Vedic Goddesses can be grouped as Avataras of the five main Goddesses of Hinduism, who are 1. Parvati (with Gauri, Annapurna etc. as Avataras), 2. Kali (with Chinnamasta, Tara etc. as Avataras), 3. Durga (with Shakambari, Katyayani etc. as Avataras), 4. Lakshmi (with Kamala, Tulasi etc. as Avataras) and 5. Sarasvati (with Gayatri, Matangi etc. as Avataras).<br /> <br /> Now, whether you treat the Avataras as alternate identities of these five, or as minor Goddesses in their own right, is an ST call. Both are valid interpretations, but I prefer the former.<br /> <br /> (Incidentally, if you're still interested in a Scion of Durga designed for 1e, who is very much her own Goddess in myth, her associateds would probably be Charisma, Appearance, Strength, Guardian and War. The abilities would be Melee, Control, Thrown, Fortitude, Presence and Empathy).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-13T09:52:44-08:00","id":48260,"title":"","content":"Speaking from personal experience, I'd say 'both are true'. The gods and goddesses are aspects of a single entity as far as the Hindu religion is concerned, and yet are still their own people, with their own individuality and personality. They don't have to be a hive mind - they are Shakti, and they are themselves. This isn't so hard to grasp, for me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1976-tashiro","name":"Tashiro","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1976_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-13T10:07:09-08:00","id":48280,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tashiro</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/38627-shiva-and-kali-aren-t-evil?p=48260#post48260\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Speaking from personal experience, I'd say 'both are true'. The gods and goddesses are aspects of a single entity as far as the Hindu religion is concerned, and yet are still their own people, with their own individuality and personality. They don't have to be a hive mind - they are Shakti, and they are themselves. This isn't so hard to grasp, for me.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, both are definitely true in the eyes of the Hindu Gods. I was just speculating as to how best this mindset may be represented mechanically in a game setting which can only do so much to model the vast diversity of mythology <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-13T22:20:53-08:00","id":48733,"title":"","content":"The easiest way is to simply say this is true. The gods are their own entities, <u>and</u> aspects of the greater god. Mechanically, it means nothing. Alternatively, you can say that they are avatars of the greater god, but are themselves as well. Much like in 1E, with the Titans, and their avatars, the most powerful of the gods can do the same thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1976-tashiro","name":"Tashiro","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1976_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-13T22:23:18-08:00","id":48736,"title":"","content":"Actually, also, you can take a page from Percy Jackson. A god can 'flip' roles when needed. 'I am Zeus of the Greek Pantheon. Now I am Jupiter of the Roman Pantheon'. Or, if you want a Japanese example: 'I am O-Inari, the Goddess / God of Rice and Foxes (and a <u>crapton of other things</u>). Now I am Saraswati, of the Hindu Pantheon, and now I am Benzaiten, of the Buddhist faith'. It also gets interesting when you look at the Egyptian / Greek crossovers, where a god of one pantheon is accepted as an aspect of a god from another pantheon.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1976-tashiro","name":"Tashiro","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1976_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"40"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}